{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-333280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2}-z,\\, & y(-2)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2-y+2z,\\, & z(-2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-333280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-2$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-2)=0$ and $z(-2)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+{y}^{2}-z=-2+{0}^{2}-1=-3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+2z=2-0+2\\cdot 1=4, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-3)\\cdot 1=-3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-2+1=-1, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-3)=-3, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $-1$ | $-3$ |  |  | $5$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+{y}^{2}-z=-1+(-3)^{2}-5=3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+2z=2-(-3)+2\\cdot 5=15, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=15\\cdot 1=15, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+3=0, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=5+15=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $-1$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ | $15$ | $15$ |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(0)≈0$\nand\n$z(0)≈20.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-333280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "858e2a599e304e8ec9cb98c5e2f7b7a83c6c324c632f011f68e975a1c5ec2940"} {"question" "q-333280" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-333280" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-333280" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-333280" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=xy+z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x-y+2,\\, & z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=0\\cdot 1+0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=0-1+2=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=\\frac{1}{2}-1+2=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "858e2a599e304e8ec9cb98c5e2f7b7a83c6c324c632f011f68e975a1c5ec2940"} {"question" "q-335115" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335115" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335115" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335115" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2y-z+x,\\, & y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′}=z+w,\\, & z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=\\frac{xz}{2y},\\, & w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y-z+x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =z+w \\\\  & =1+(-2) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =\\frac{xz}{2y} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot 1}{2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{16}=\\frac{11}{16} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{3}{4})=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=-2+\\frac{3}{16}=-\\frac{29}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $-2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{11}{16}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{29}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{11}{16},\\,z(1)≈\\frac{1}{4},\\,w(1)≈-\\frac{29}{16}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "858e2a599e304e8ec9cb98c5e2f7b7a83c6c324c632f011f68e975a1c5ec2940"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335286" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{29}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=1+x-2y+3z,\\, & y(0)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2y-w,\\, & z(0)=1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=-x+yz,\\, & w(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-6}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=0,$ $z(0)=1,$ and $w(0)=1,$ we place $y=0,$ $z=1,$ and $w=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1+x-2y+3z \\\\  & =1+0-2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2y-w \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-x+yz \\\\  & =-0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈1,$\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈\\frac{3}{4},$\nand\n$w\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "858e2a599e304e8ec9cb98c5e2f7b7a83c6c324c632f011f68e975a1c5ec2940"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e9094