86/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335318" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=2-x+z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=3y-2z+w,\\, & z(0)=-1 \\\\ {w}^{′}=1-y,\\, & w(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-4}},\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-5}},\nand $w\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈$\n{{field-6}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1,$ $z(0)=-1,$ and $w(0)=0,$ we place $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ and $w=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},{z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2-x+z \\\\  & =2-0+(-1) \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3y-2z+w \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)+0 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {w}^{′} & =1-y \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $-1$ | $5$ |  | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+1=0 \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{6}{5}$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈\\frac{6}{5},$\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈0,$\nand\n$w\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/state.json" "file_sha256" "e1adfecee212721c302f80f198a20085e909412d81c79a80cc4909d1fcd06c94"} {"question" "q-335319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068386/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "858e2a599e304e8ec9cb98c5e2f7b7a83c6c324c632f011f68e975a1c5ec2940"} {"question" "q-